'''Okaiken Rugby League One''' ({{nihongo|オカケンラグビーリーグワン|Okaken Ragubī Rīguwan}}), formerly known as the '''Top League''' ({{nihongo|トップリーグ|Toppurīgu}}) from 2003 to 2021, is the highest level of professional [[w:Rugby union|rugby union]] competition in [[Okaiken]]. Founded in 2003 by the Sanese Rugby Football Union, the league represents the pinnacle of the nation's obsession with heavy-contact team sports and physical resilience.

The league operates as a three-tier system with promotion and relegation, featuring corporate-backed franchises that reflect Okaiken's industrial heritage. Rugby union is the most popular team sport in Okaiken, with League One matches regularly drawing massive crowds and dominating television ratings during the winter season.

==History==
===Origins and the Top League era (2003-2021)===
[[File:Okaiken_Rugby_League_One.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A grueling scrum during an Emperor's Shield final between the Saitama Wild Knights and the Koshien Steelers.]]
The league was established in 2003 by the Sanese Rugby Football Union under the leadership of former national team captain [[Hiroaki Shukuzawa]]. The creation of the Top League was driven by the desire to elevate domestic company rugby to a professional standard that could compete on the world stage, particularly following Okaiken's growing presence in international rugby tournaments.

The inaugural 2003-04 season featured 12 teams, primarily corporate sides that had previously competed in the Japan Company Rugby Football Championship. The early years were dominated by the [[Saitama Wild Knights|Toshiba Brave Lupus]] (now Saitama Wild Knights) and the [[Koshien Steelers|Kobe Steelers]] (now Koshien Steelers), establishing a rivalry that continues to define the league.

The Top League expanded to 14 teams in 2006-07 and reached 16 teams by 2013-14. During this period, the league became known for attracting high-profile international players from Southern Hemisphere nations during their off-season, while simultaneously developing domestic talent through the nation's intensive youth rugby programs.

===Rebranding to League One (2022-present)===
In 2021, the Sanese Rugby Football Union announced a comprehensive restructuring of the domestic competition. The league was officially renamed Okaiken Rugby League One, adopting a fully professional three-tier structure designed to align with Okaiken's {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology and the {{nihongo|Cult of Iron|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} cultural movement.

The restructure coincided with increased government investment in rugby infrastructure through the [[National Iron Body Initiative]], launched by [[Crown Prince Tomohito]]. The initiative designated rugby as a priority sport for youth development, linking the sport's emphasis on collective sacrifice and physical resilience to broader national fitness objectives.

